 "action_summary": "The owner asked why the machine cannot simply be given coding experiences and learn from them. Traced on main: the learner exists and works when driven directly (the repair row with its own retained_learning setting fixed one bug by search, kept the lesson, and fixed the next six differently shaped instances of the same mistake from memory in one check each, all passing an independent audit), but the front door refuses every request that would run code with FAIL_CLOSED__COMPUTER_EXECUTION_REQUIRES_ADMITTED_CONFINEMENT, and OPTIMAL_PLAN_20261103_V1 counts that one status as 226 of the 233 failures behind the door. The door is closed because the only carrier in the tree is not qualified, and carrier_qualification_probe_v1 has reported two objections still standing since 2026-11-03: no syscall filter, and the worker runs the host's libc. This change removes the first as far as a producer can. One module declares a list (193 calls allowed, 5 allowed only in a harmless form, 63 refused by name with EPERM, every other call answered ENOSYS so the C library falls back, a caller that switches instruction set killed) and assembles it into a classic-BPF seccomp program with no dependency, byte-identical on every x86_64 host. The launcher installs it in the step that enters the worker's root, before env, setpriv and the workload; if it cannot be installed nothing runs. The probe stops searching the launcher's source for a word and asks the worker instead. No capability is added, no row is registered, the front door stays closed, carrier_qualified stays False, and the list is the producer's: whether it is the right list is a review this change cannot perform.",
